# Conventional Commits

```text
type(optional-scope): subject

optional body

optional footer
```

- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`. A speed or memory change is `feat` or `fix` with its measurement in the body; there is no `perf` type.
- Scope: English, a short noun for the area. Examples, not a closed list: `core`, `data`, `lod`, `tiles`, `spec`, `scale`, `render`, `gpu`, `d3d11`, `shaders`, `interaction`, `wpf`, `testing`, `bench`, `rules`, `deps`.
- Subject: English, imperative, no trailing period. The type and scope are always English (`.cursor/rules/language.mdc`).
- Breaking change: `type(scope)!: subject` and/or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer.
- A closing keyword (`Closes #N`) never goes in a commit body; the squash message concatenates commit bodies (`.cursor/rules/github-closing.mdc`). Write `Refs #N` or `Part of #N`.
- Every commit takes one of the types above, the ones in the middle of a row too. There is no `wip`: `wip(...)` commits leave the owner to rewrite the squash message.

```text
feat(lod): build the min/max pyramid of a column chunk on a worker
fix(d3d11): release every back-buffer view before ResizeBuffers
chore(rules): record the chunk-boundary rule from the retrospective
```

```text
# BAD — wip(data): more chunk work
# GOOD — feat(data): map column chunks from a file
```
